    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-dim)]">Invited undergraduate project · September–December 2021</p>
    <h1>Reading difficulties in primary-school children</h1>
    <p>
      While studying philosophy at Shenzhen University, I was invited by my teacher, Dong Li,
      to join a project with the Shenzhen Learning Disorders Association and Yucai No. 2 Primary
      School. It gave me early experience of behavioural assessment and raised questions about
      how children connect written characters with pronunciation and meaning.
    </p>

    <h2>Tasks and my contribution</h2>
    <p>
      I helped select Chinese characters and prepare PowerPoint slides presenting one character
      at a time. The project involved timed reading tasks and accuracy records. Children were
      tested in batches, and I took turns participating in on-site testing and analysing the
      collected response data. My recollection is that timing was recorded for a set of
      characters, rather than separately for each spoken response.
    </p>
    <p>
      My participation also included online observation of assessments. The wider assessment
      covered single-character and word reading, as well as visual discrimination and pattern
      tasks. I also helped prepare teaching items and individual learning plans within the project.
    </p>

    <h2>An observation from the assessment</h2>
    <p>
      In a reflection emailed on 13 September 2021, I described watching two teachers assess
      a child online. The child initially read relatively simple, common characters in isolation.
      When the teachers later presented words, the child recognised more characters than I
      had anticipated from the earlier single-character performance.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        The contrast that caught my attention was the same kind of written unit encountered
        alone versus within a familiar word: could word context help a child retrieve a
        pronunciation that was difficult to access in isolation?
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      This is a paraphrase of the question I raised in that reflection, not a controlled
      comparison using matched items. It led me to consider how familiarity and context
      might support the connection between written form and sound.
    </p>

    <h2>What this experience led me to ask</h2>
    <p>
      A reading response depends on more than whether a child has encountered a character
      before. I became interested in which parts of the task help them access what they know,
      and how an assessment can distinguish difficulty with a representation from difficulty
      retrieving it in a particular context. This remains relevant to my interest in language
      understanding and the interpretation of behavioural evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page documents my undergraduate contribution and a contemporaneous observation.
      The original response data are no longer available, so I report no numerical findings
      or diagnostic conclusions here.
    </p>
